    "question": {
      ":question/id": "095495f3-e290-511e-ad13-c6259f6c1165",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following matrices are **not** in row echelon form?\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a matrix is in row echelon form if it satisfies the following conditions:\n\nI. All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n\nII. Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is not in row echelon form since the first row contains only zeros and it is not at the bottom of the matrix (condition I is violated).\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -9 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is not in row echelon form since the second row contains only zeros and it is not at the bottom of the matrix (condition I is violated).\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -7 & -6 & 4 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 9 & 4 & 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 8 & 9 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $A$ and $B$ are **not** in row echelon form.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "9eeb596b-3d4d-5dbf-b7b4-22948022f64b",
