    "question": {
      ":question/id": "b3d8b4ee-e99f-518a-9998-35a82412de53",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following matrices are **not** in row echelon form?\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a matrix is in row echelon form if it satisfies the following conditions:\n\nI. All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n\nII. Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is not in row echelon form since the leading entry $a_{21}=6$ is to the left of the leading entry $a_{12}=2$ (condition II is violated).\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is not in row echelon form since the leading entry $b_{22}=4$ is to the left of the leading entry $b_{13}=8$ (condition II is violated).\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 8 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -7 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is not in row echelon form since the first row contains only zeros and it is not at the bottom of the matrix (condition I is violated).\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ are **not** in row echelon form.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
