    "question": {
      ":question/id": "e53ca2ec-17e0-5453-87e5-d0c02fc3a29e",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following matrices are **not** in row echelon form?\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a matrix is in row echelon form if it satisfies the following conditions:\n\nI. All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n\nII. Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is not in row echelon form since the leading entry $a_{33}=-2$ is in the same column as the leading entry $a_{23}=-9$ (condition II is violated).\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 3 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -9 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is not in row echelon form since the leading entry $c_{23}=6$ is in the same column as the leading entry $c_{13}=-9$ (condition II is violated).\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $A$ and $C$ are **not** in row echelon form.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
